## Prong vs Bezel: Which Setting Survives Daily Wear?

This is where most buyers make the wrong decision.

Prong-set eternity bands look brilliant in a jewelry case. Each stone is raised slightly, catching light from multiple angles. But prongs on an eternity band are tiny — the band surface has no room for the larger four-prong mountings you see on solitaires. You are dealing with micro-prongs.

Micro-prongs on an eternity band snag fabric, catch hair, and bend from daily contact more easily than the prongs on an engagement ring solitaire. On a solitaire, one center stone has four or six robust prongs. On an eternity band, each of the 12–20 small stones has two to four micro-prongs, totaling 24–80 prong tips around the band.

Bezel-set eternity bands encase each stone in a continuous metal rim. Nothing protrudes. The band surface is smooth. You can wear it while cooking, working out, doing dishes — the metal rim protects the stone and the stone protects the rim.

The tradeoff: a bezel setting blocks light from entering the stone's sides and pavilion. Less light in means less brilliance and fire compared to a prong setting. A bezel radiant eternity band still sparkles — but it sparkles differently from a prong version. The flash is softer and more controlled.

For active daily wear, bezel wins. For occasional wear where brilliance is the priority, prong is the right call.

> "Half the customers who contact us about damaged eternity bands are prong-set. I always ask — do you wear it every day while working? If yes, bezel. If it comes off for the gym and dishes, prong is fine." — Farzana

