## Metal Guide: All Four Options

White gold is the most popular choice for radiant cut eternity bands. The cool-toned metal recedes visually, making the rectangle diamonds pop. It needs rhodium re-plating every 1–3 years to maintain the bright white finish.

Yellow gold warms the look of the band. It does not need re-plating. It pairs naturally with vintage-style engagement rings and with warm skin tones. The tradeoff is that yellow gold can make H-I color stones look slightly warmer than they would in white gold — a minor effect in small eternity stones, not usually visible.

Rose gold is the least common but the most distinctive choice. The blush-pink tone works well with feminine ring stacks and with round or pear-shaped engagement rings. Rose gold is the most durable gold alloy because copper (the metal that creates the pink color) hardens the alloy.

Platinum is the premium choice. It is heavier, denser, and more scratch-resistant than gold alloys. It develops a natural patina over time rather than losing metal — scratches displace material rather than removing it, so platinum bands last longer without thinning. For a daily-wear eternity band where durability is the priority, platinum is worth the price difference.

