## Carat Weight Guide: What Each Size Looks Like

Carat weight in an eternity band works differently than in a solitaire.

A 1ct solitaire is one diamond. A 1ct eternity band is 8–14 small diamonds spread around the finger. The visual read is completely different.

**Under 2ct total weight:** The band reads as delicate. Good for stacking with other bands. Can feel thin on fingers wider than a size 7. Rarely the first choice for a standalone statement band.

**2–3ct total weight:** The standard range. Most fingers in sizes 4–7 look proportional with a 2–3ct eternity band. The Rectangle Flow Effect is visible but not overwhelming. A [lab 2ct at $2,676](https://www.bluenile.com/wedding-rings/radiant-cut-eternity-band-item-262962?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) or [3ct at $3,000](https://www.bluenile.com/wedding-rings/radiant-cut-eternity-band-item-262694?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) both land here.

**4–5ct total weight:** The band makes a clear statement. You notice it immediately. It pairs best with a slim engagement ring so the two pieces do not compete. On larger hands (size 8+), this is often the proportional starting point.

**6–7ct total weight:** Maximum coverage. The Rectangle Flow Effect is at its strongest — the band looks like a continuous diamond surface, not individual stones. A [lab 7ct at $4,860](https://www.bluenile.com/wedding-rings/radiant-cut-eternity-band-item-263166?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) delivers this look without the natural price premium.

**General rule:** Choose carat weight by finger size and style goal, not by price alone. A 3ct on a size 5 finger looks very different from a 3ct on a size 9. If you are between sizes in both ring size and carat weight, go up on ring size and down on carat — fit is more important than width.

