## Color and Clarity: Different Rules Than Solitaires

Most buyers approach eternity band color and clarity grades using the same logic as solitaires. That is a mistake that costs money.

A 3ct solitaire has one large center stone. A 3ct radiant eternity band has approximately 12–16 stones, each around 0.18–0.25ct. Human eyes evaluate color and clarity very differently at 0.20ct versus 2ct.

**Color:** In a small eternity stone, the color difference between F and H is nearly invisible to the naked eye. The lab bands in the table above use F-G color — a high standard for lab diamonds where the price premium over H-I is small. The natural bands use H-I color, which is the sweet spot for small eternity stones — white enough to look clean, low enough to keep the price reasonable.

**Clarity:** SI1-SI2 is the right clarity grade for eternity band stones. At 0.20ct per stone, SI1-SI2 inclusions are well below the threshold of naked-eye visibility. VS clarity is unnecessary in small eternity stones and adds cost without visual benefit.

**The certified rule:** Always confirm that the grading certificate covers the full parcel of stones, not just one representative stone. A reputable vendor provides parcel-level grading for eternity bands. The data on this page comes from certified market inventory with parcel-level grading.

> "I had a client who upgraded her eternity band clarity from SI1 to VVS2 — spent $2,000 extra. Her friends cannot tell the difference. Neither can I. For eternity bands, clarity above SI1 is vanity, not quality." — Farzana

